April 16, 2007 Salem, Massachusetts By Mike Cintolo --------------------- Featuring Mike's Musings: Hello Again! Income funds yielding 8% to 10% A New Market Timing Vehicle The King of Video Games -------------------- The last time I wrote to you, Tim was in China, the market had just taken a hard right hook to the jaw, and I was thrown into duty as your honorary Cabot Wealth Advisory writer. Well, apparently the Grand Poobah (i.e., Tim) was so happy with mine and Paul's write-ups that we're now on spot CWA duty. (That's right: I did a good job writing ... so now I have to do more work. Gotta love it!) Seriously though, you'll be getting my (and, separately, Paul's) latest thoughts once a month from here on, giving Tim's fingers and brain a chance to recharge ... and giving us a chance to ad-lib things a little bit. For my part, I hope you enjoy my ideas, but whether you do or not, all comments are welcome - just reply to this letter, or give me a shout at mike@cabotwealth.com ----- For this issue of the CWA, I'm going to attempt to provide something every reader wants - methods and tools that can make you a better investor, and hence, will help you make more money over time. Now, I could list off a couple of top-notch stocks, but heck, Tim does that in most issues. So in this space I'm going to chat about a couple of newer, unique investment vehicles, and one little-followed investment tool, that are great ways to accumulate profits. Fair warning: The first topic I'm going to write about concerns income-oriented securities. If you're a true-blue growth-stock investor, skip down to the next section. But if you're a bit older, maybe looking for a couple of higher-yielding ideas, stick around. I'm not even 30 years young at this point, so I still have a shred of that youthful exuberance we all start life with. And that means my investments tend to be aggressive, figuring I've got plenty of time (God willing!) to make up for most monetary stumbles. However, things do change, and as I move through life (I just got engaged two and a half weeks ago!) I realize those conservative old fogies I used to snicker at actually had some great ideas, after all. Even so, most bond-type investments don't appeal to me. In my mind, I can get 5% in a zero-risk money market fund. So what's the use of jumping into a bond that yields, say, 6%? Am I really going to lock my money into something that gives me an extra 1%? No thank you. Yet with the advent of exchange traded funds (dubbed ETFs), some new, innovative high-yielding stuff has come on the market, including a batch of covered call funds. These are basically mutual funds that invest in a bunch of big, steady, dividend-paying companies, and then sell call options against their holdings. For the uninitiated, selling a call option means you're "betting" the stock will fall ... but by owning the underlying stock, you're also "betting" the stock will rise. The result is a hedged position, where you're collecting money by selling the call option, but also having a chance of participating in some upside. Industry jargon aside, the bottom line is that some very respectable fund companies offer these covered call funds (Eaton Vance, Blackstone, Nuveen, etc.), most pay dividends monthly or quarterly, and most have annual yields in the 8% to 10% range (before taxes). Of course, the funds themselves trade like stocks, and they can rise and fall depending on the market, or interest rates. In other words, there's no free lunch. Even so, these funds provide a steady stream of income over time, using a method (covered-call writing) that's been proven to work for many years. So if you're looking for a solid, higher-yielding income investment, give them a look; some of the symbols include JLA, JSN, EOS and EEF, but there are many others. I believe many income-seeking investors would benefit by putting some money to work there. --- >From the world of income-oriented securities, we now switch gears to market timi